    {
      "id": "9a48ce9e795aff4d4d83d16996090617079015cd",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1207820940,
          "type": null,
          "body": "The change to PLATFORM eliminates a sanity check. I'm not sure why you think that is a good idea.\n\nThe change to WIRESHARK_BASE_DIR: build dirs are transient and in general not an appropriate default target for wireshark support libraries. They should reside outside the build tree, since they're not a build product.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2639362,
            "username": "jvalverde",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "João Valverde",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bea5f745397cf5a2fe0526df96ec143db47f9334340ab56515038832be57e976?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/jvalverde"
          },
          "created_at": "2022-12-13T16:38:10.448Z",
          "updated_at": "2022-12-13T16:38:10.448Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 192605357,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 9018,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "47a59d94c85bbf81cca8df091564f26186ae6701",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1209276847,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> The change to WIRESHARK_BASE_DIR: build dirs are transient and in general not an appropriate default target for wireshark support libraries. They should reside outside the build tree, since they're not a build product.\n\nIn general I agree and therefore it is good that there is the possibility to customize the value. On the other hand it makes life easier when you want to build Wireshark with Visual Studio Code and its CMake extension.\n\n> The change to PLATFORM eliminates a sanity check. I'm not sure why you think that is a good idea.\n\nWhat kind of sanity check? Did I miss something?\n\nI think x64 as Platform is a reasonable assumption as a default for Windows machines these days.",
          "author": {
            "id": 7625625,
            "username": "Developer-Alexander",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Developer Alexander",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/81b3f01484e58a678d9a77825dd6726e0e7e2383b67d9a16215a35fe0a729529?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/Developer-Alexander"
          },
          "created_at": "2022-12-14T14:34:42.475Z",
          "updated_at": "2022-12-14T14:34:42.475Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 192605357,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 9018,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "61cb924e5e9b955e65509f915fc29e8fbf5d3827",
      "individual_note": false,
      "resolvable": true,
      "resolved": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1209356246,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "> What kind of sanity check? Did I miss something?\n\nI believe PLATFORM is set (or not) by the visual studio bat files and the intent is to check for mismatches.\n\n> In general I agree and therefore it is good that there is the possibility to customize the value. On the other hand it makes life easier when you want to build Wireshark with Visual Studio Code and its CMake extension.\n\nOK. I don´t have any experience with that so I can't offer much help but seems like the problem is with Visual Studio and not our lack of an (unsuitable) default.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2639362,
            "username": "jvalverde",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "João Valverde",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bea5f745397cf5a2fe0526df96ec143db47f9334340ab56515038832be57e976?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/jvalverde"
          },
          "created_at": "2022-12-14T15:17:36.252Z",
          "updated_at": "2022-12-14T20:13:51.317Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 192605357,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 9018,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 1209788553,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "> I believe PLATFORM is set (or not) by the visual studio bat files and the intent is to check for mismatches.\n\nThat's correct; if the user is building from an \"x64 Native Tools Command Prompt for VS 2022\" command prompt or ran `vcvarsall.bat x64` then `Platform` will be set to `x64`. Otherwise it will be missing or set to some other architecture.\n\n>> I think x64 as Platform is a reasonable assumption as a default for Windows machines these days.\n\nIs it? Opening a \"Developer Command Prompt for VS2022\" window on my Windows VM here gives me an environment that targets x86 with an unset `Platform` environment variable.\n\nIt's better to make it immediately obvious that there's an issue with the VS environment rather than let the user get too far into the build (thereby wasting their time) which might necessitate reaching out for support for a possibly-non-obvious error (thereby wasting everyone's time).",
          "author": {
            "id": 860017,
            "username": "geraldcombs",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Gerald Combs",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://gitlab.com/uploads/-/system/user/avatar/860017/avatar.png?v=1789801377",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/geraldcombs"
          },
          "created_at": "2022-12-14T20:13:51.235Z",
          "updated_at": "2022-12-14T20:13:51.235Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 192605357,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 9018,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 1210450702,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "I typically do not use the `Native Tools Command Prompt for VS`. I typically use a regular console or some tools like Visual Studio Code to generate the project and it actually works fine besides the aspect that the wireshark project requires some environment variables to be set.\n\nI think it is a good idea to have these options. But I also think that it would be great if a user could build wireshark in a regular console with doing nothing more than:\n```\nmkdir build\ncd build\ncmake ..\n```\nUnfortunately this is not possible at the moment. In my opinion it would also save a lot of time if it would be possible to go this way.",
          "author": {
            "id": 7625625,
            "username": "Developer-Alexander",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Developer Alexander",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/81b3f01484e58a678d9a77825dd6726e0e7e2383b67d9a16215a35fe0a729529?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/Developer-Alexander"
          },
          "created_at": "2022-12-15T10:09:18.502Z",
          "updated_at": "2022-12-15T10:09:18.502Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 192605357,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 9018,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 1210500769,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Using a correct VS environment is a requirement from the compiler vendor, so that's what the project recommends and supports. So the PLATFORM change will not be merged. Relevant VS Code [documentation](https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/cpp/config-msvc)\n\nHaving to configure two environment variables (base dir and Qt path) is hardly a burden. This can easily be automated with a one-time setup for any build environment. Still personally I would not object to a reasonable default for `WIRESHARK_BASE_DIR` like `C:\\Development`.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2639362,
            "username": "jvalverde",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "João Valverde",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bea5f745397cf5a2fe0526df96ec143db47f9334340ab56515038832be57e976?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/jvalverde"
          },
          "created_at": "2022-12-15T10:37:10.073Z",
          "updated_at": "2022-12-15T10:37:10.073Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 192605357,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 9018,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 1389544803,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "What exactly is `WIRESHARK_BASE_DIR` intended for?\n\nLet's say I want to have multiple instances of the Wireshark repository on my disk. I would assume that setting `WIRESHARK_BASE_DIR` globally to one of them would not work for the others, right?\n\nWould it be a problem to have the use the current source directory as default for `WIRESHARK_BASE_DIR` when generation with cmake?",
          "author": {
            "id": 7625625,
            "username": "Developer-Alexander",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Developer Alexander",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/81b3f01484e58a678d9a77825dd6726e0e7e2383b67d9a16215a35fe0a729529?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/Developer-Alexander"
          },
          "created_at": "2023-05-14T18:22:08.539Z",
          "updated_at": "2023-05-14T18:22:32.449Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 192605357,
