        {
          "id": 1389574595,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "> What exactly is `WIRESHARK_BASE_DIR` intended for?\n\nTo set the filesystem location for Wireshark support libraries on Windows.\n\n> Let's say I want to have multiple instances of the Wireshark repository on my disk. I would assume that setting `WIRESHARK_BASE_DIR` globally to one of them would not work for the others, right?\n\nYou can point multiple repositories to the same location.\n\n> Would it be a problem to have the use the current source directory as default for `WIRESHARK_BASE_DIR` when generation with cmake?\n\nI'm not sure.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2639362,
            "username": "jvalverde",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "João Valverde",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bea5f745397cf5a2fe0526df96ec143db47f9334340ab56515038832be57e976?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/jvalverde"
          },
          "created_at": "2023-05-14T21:50:46.701Z",
          "updated_at": "2023-05-14T21:50:46.701Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 192605357,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 9018,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 1391051733,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "`WIRESHARK_BASE_DIR` exists because Windows has no standard package manager suitable for our use[1]. As a result, we've had to roll our own. The Wireshark sources don't contain a top-level item named wireshark-win64-libs, so there shouldn't be an issue with setting `WIRESHARK_BASE_DIR` to the source directory.\n\n[1]Yes, vcpkg and NuGet exist but neither lets you run a series of commands that will install our dependencies like the various Linux distributions or Homebrew.",
          "author": {
            "id": 860017,
            "username": "geraldcombs",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Gerald Combs",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://gitlab.com/uploads/-/system/user/avatar/860017/avatar.png?v=1789801377",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/geraldcombs"
          },
